 but you can see that it blew up on line five,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.360
 it blew up right here on line five.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.080
 So what we want to do is we want it to say one, two, three,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.240
 done, but we want it to detect that we've typed in done,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.480
 so here we'll just say if the string value

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.040
 that I got back from input is double equal,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.160
 quote, done, quote, break.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.940
 So that basically will break us out,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.940
 and now print all done.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.100
 I should be using single quotes here.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.360
 Too much Java coding.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.220
 Print all done, then I'm going to say print,

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:54.980
 what do I want to print, the total, the num,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:59.980
 and then tot, comma, num, comma, tot, overnum.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:06.680
 Now we've got to be careful,

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.080
 because we don't want to divide by zero,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.220
 but that'll get us sort of a ways.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.460
 So this is going to run, it's going to read these things,

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.460
 it's going to accumulate here.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.880
 This is the accumulator pattern.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.480
 This is a counter pattern

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.260
 where we're adding one to a current variable,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.460
 an accumulator pattern where we're adding a value to it.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:27.640
 So now we should be able to see the done.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.680
 Four, five, and six, and then done,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.600
 and the total of four plus five plus six is 15.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.880
 The number is three, and the average is five,

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.000
 and so that's really good.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.720
 The all done prints out.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.160
 I just did that for yucks,

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.440
 and you can see the value that's coming out.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.580
 So that's in pretty good shape.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.000
 So I'm going to comment this out, and comment that out.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.720
 So this is pretty good.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.560
 It works just the way we want it to work.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.160
 Four, five, six, but if we do something

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.800
 other than we're done,

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.240
 then we're going to blow up in this float.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.360
 And so this is where we're going to have to do a try and accept,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.080
 because we just know that this line, line seven,

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.820
 line seven is the danger zone, okay?

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.160
 So what we're going to do is we're going to put a try in here,

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.220
 and then we're going to indent the part of code

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.560
 that seems strange,

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:23.760
 and then we're going to have some accept code.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.980
 And the first thing we have to do in the accept code

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.800
 is print out the word invalid input.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.220
 Come back.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:40.200
 Print invalid input.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.960
 Now, just like in an earlier example,

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.480
 we have to do something here

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.360
 to make sure it doesn't just keep on going,

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.320
 because Fval doesn't work.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:51.760
 We're not going to see the error message

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.060
 that would be the traceback here on line nine.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.200
 We're going to run here, but we still don't want to add,

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.620
 because Fval will be.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.360
 So this is where we can use the continue.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.020
 So in this code, we're using both the break

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.160
 to say if I'm all done break,

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.400
 and if I have a problem, I'll print a message out,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.580
 and then I'll say continue.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.080
 So the continue basically says go back up to the top.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.200
 So that is how, when we see enter some bad data,

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:18.960
 we print out invalid input,

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.600
 and without adding anything new,

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.160
 you don't really see it here,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.920
 without adding anything new,

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.280
 you go back up to the top and enter a second thing.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.760
 So now, if everything is right,

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.400
 I should be able to type bad input.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:40.640
 Four, five, six, bad input, bad input, done.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.320
 And I have a total of 15 and three items,

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.820
 and the average is 5.0.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:49.840
 So there we go.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.920
 That's what we're going to get,

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.480
 and that roughly achieves this same thing,

00:07:55.480 --> 00:08:00.480
 and it's a combination of a loop with a exit mechanism.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.760
 We have some sanity checking of our input,

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.560
 so making sure that we have some valid input,

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.440
 and we catch it, and we use continue to loop back up

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.560
 to run the next iteration of the loop,

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:15.320
 and we have an accumulator pattern,

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.920
 and then we can use the accumulated data

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.600
 to print what we want to print.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:24.120
 So I hope that this has been useful to you.

